## At work

Most people with fatty liver disease are well and work normally. You may need time off for scans and appointments.

## Is it a disability?

Early fatty liver disease is **unlikely** to count as a disability. Cirrhosis that causes fatigue, confusion or other symptoms may be covered.

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**.

## Benefits

Fatty liver disease on its own does not usually qualify for PIP. Advanced liver disease, with severe tiredness, confusion or fluid build-up, may affect activities such as **preparing food**, **washing**, **moving around** and **managing medicines**.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.
